| #465 must land first \u2014 ctx methods require async context |\\n| 3 | Target files identified | AVAILABLE | backlog_core/server.py, operations.py |\\n| 4 | MCP prompts API understood | AVAILABLE | @mcp.prompt, list[Message] return type, verified in docs |\\n| 5 | ctx.elicit() API understood | AVAILABLE | async method, dataclass response_type, ElicitationResult |\\n| 6 | Skills to replace identified | AVAILABLE | /backlog, /create-backlog-item, /groom-backlog-item, /work-backlog-item (partially) |\\n| 7 | Guided tools spec complete | AVAILABLE | 4 tools fully described with input/output behavior |\\n\\nDecision: APPROVED \u2014 item is well-specified. Prerequisites are external blockers (#472, #465), not missing information.\\nMissing inputs: #472 merged, #465 merged (both open as of 2026-03-06).\\n</details>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-05-24T03:25:42.512439Z</sub>\\n\\nDate: 2026-05-23\\nGoal: Consolidate backlog workflow logic into the MCP server so it travels with the tools and stays current without skill updates.\\n\\n| # | Condition | Status | Info Needed |\\n|---|-----------|--------|-------------|\\n| 1 | FastMCP async tool support (#472 merged) | AVAILABLE | Closed: commits a89eb9d9 (async conversion) and 74a913ed (resolve #472 mark complete) confirmed 2026-05-23 |\\n| 2 | Context logging (#465 merged) | AVAILABLE | Closed: commits 895d61e3 (post-impl annotations) and a89eb9d9 confirmed 2026-05-23 |\\n| 3 | Target files identified | AVAILABLE | plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py (path corrected from stale .claude/skills/backlog/ reference) |\\n| 4 | MCP prompts API understood | AVAILABLE | @mcp.prompt, list[Message] return type, verified in FastMCP docs |\\n| 5 | ctx.elicit() API understood | AVAILABLE | async method, dataclass response_type, ElicitationResult \u2014 3 action outcomes (accept/decline/cancel) |\\n| 6 | Skills to replace identified | AVAILABLE | /backlog, /create-backlog-item, /groom-backlog-item, /work-backlog-item (partially) |\\n| 7 | Guided tools spec complete | AVAILABLE | 5 prompts + 4 guided tools fully described with input/output behavior |\\n| 8 | #2292 server consolidation sequencing | DERIVABLE | #2292 (40\u21924 consolidated tools, open, in-progress) restructures server.py; must determine whether #473 must land before or after #2292 to avoid rebase cost |\\n\\nAVAILABLE count: 7\\nDERIVABLE count: 1\\nMISSING count: 0\\n\\nDecision: APPROVED \u2014 all blocking prerequisites (#472, #465) are confirmed closed. One derivable condition (#2292 sequencing) can be resolved by reading the #2292 scope. Item is clear to proceed once sequencing is confirmed.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-03-06)\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n8/10 \u2014 Consolidates scattered workflow logic into the MCP server, making it client-independent and eliminating version skew between skills and tools. The /backlog, /create-backlog-item, /groom-backlog-item, and /work-backlog-item skills currently duplicate logic that must be kept in sync with the MCP server manually. Moving this logic into the server means any MCP client (Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Cursor, Zed) gets the full workflow without requiring skill installation or updates.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Workflow logic currently lives in 4 Claude Code skills \u2014 those skills are Claude Code-only; MCP prompts work across all MCP clients (Claude Desktop, Cursor, Zed, etc.)\\n- Duplicate intake/confirmation flows in skills and server drift over time as either side evolves\\n- Users on non-Claude-Code MCP clients have no guided workflow today \u2014 this unblocks them\\n- The /create-backlog-item interactive mode and /work-backlog-item setup-github command become redundant after this ships\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n**Blocked by:**\\n- **#472** (P2, open) \u2014 \\\"Convert backlog_core tools to async def\\\" \u2014 delivers `async def` tool patterns and `asyncio.to_thread()` wrapping for PyGithub calls. Required because `ctx.elicit()` is an async method; guided tools must be `async def` to call it. **Must merge first.**\\n- **#465** (P1, open) \u2014 \\\"Add Context logging and progress reporting\\\" \u2014 delivers `ctx: Context` parameter pattern established in server.py. While #465 adds `ctx` to existing tools, #473 uses `ctx` in new async tools. Can proceed independently but both items touch `server.py` \u2014 sequence to avoid merge conflicts.\\n\\n**Priority mismatch**: #472 (prerequisite) is P2 while #473 is P1. Either #472 must be bumped to P1, or #473 should be held until #472 lands.\\n\\n**Blocks:** Nothing currently in backlog.\\n\\n### Agents\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-03-06T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nImplementation: `@python3-development:python-cli-architect` \u2014 additive changes (new async functions, new imports) to server.py. No existing tools are modified.\\n\\nReview: `@python3-development:python-code-reviewer` \u2014 verify async patterns, elicitation response handling (all 3 action outcomes guarded), and that existing sync tools are untouched.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-03-30T05:13:36Z</sub>\\n\\nImplementation: `@python-engineering:python-cli-architect` \u2014 additive changes (new async functions, new imports) to server.py. No existing tools are modified.\\n\\nReview: `@python-engineering:python-code-reviewer` \u2014 verify async patterns, elicitation response handling (all 3 action outcomes guarded), and that existing sync tools are untouched.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Files\\n\\nPrimary implementation:\\n- `.claude/skills/backlog/backlog_core/server.py` \u2014 add 5 `@mcp.prompt` functions and 4 async `@mcp.tool` functions with `ctx: Context`\\n- `.claude/skills/backlog/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 read-only dependency; `view_item()`, `add_item()`, `close_item()`, `resolve_item()` called by new guided tools\\n\\nSkills to thin after implementation:\\n- `.claude/skills/backlog/SKILL.md`\\n- `.claude/skills/create-backlog-item/SKILL.md`\\n- `.claude/skills/groom-backlog-item/SKILL.md`\\n- `.claude/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md`\\n\\nFastMCP reference (read-only, for implementer):\\n- `.claude/worktrees/fastmcp/docs/servers/prompts.mdx`\\n- `.claude/worktrees/fastmcp/docs/v2/servers/elicitation.mdx`\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\nType: unbounded-design\\nScenario-target: Skills contain workflow logic (intake, grooming, working, closing flows) that could live in the MCP server, making it client-independent and always current.\\nAnalysis method: design-framing \u2014 no defect, no recurrence; this is architectural consolidation of scattered workflow logic into its natural home.\\n\\n### Output / Evidence\\n\\nObservable when done:\\n\\n1. An MCP client can call `prompts/list` and see `backlog_guide`, `create_item_guided`, `groom_item`, `work_item`, `close_or_resolve` in the response\\n2. Calling `backlog_guide` with no arguments returns message content containing the full tool reference (same content as the /backlog SKILL.md Primary Interface section)\\n3. Calling `groom_item(selector=\\\"#473\\\")` returns messages including the item's current body and grooming workflow instructions\\n4. Calling `backlog_add_guided` in an elicitation-capable client pauses and presents a form collecting `title`, `priority`, `description`, `type`\\n5. Calling `backlog_close_confirmed` presents a confirmation prompt before executing close\\n6. Calling `backlog_resolve_confirmed` presents a summary-collection form before executing resolve\\n7. All 10 existing tools remain registered and unmodified \u2014 no regressions\\n8. Guided tools handle all 3 elicitation outcomes (accept/decline/cancel) and return clear messages for each\\n9. Guided tools handle `NotSupportedError` when client does not support elicitation and return a helpful fallback message\\n\\n### Prior Work\\n\\nNo partial implementation found as of 2026-03-06. Grep for `@mcp.prompt`, `mcp.prompt`, and `elicit(` across `.claude/skills/backlog/` returned no matches. No git commits referencing \\\"mcp prompt\\\" or \\\"elicit\\\" in the backlog_core area. server.py contains only 10 synchronous `def` tools \u2014 no prompts, no `ctx` usage, no `async def`.\\n\\n### Research\\n\\n**FastMCP prompts API** \u2014 SOURCE: `.claude/worktrees/fastmcp/docs/servers/prompts.mdx` (accessed 2026-03-06)\\n\\nImport path:\\n```python\\nfrom fastmcp.prompts import Message, PromptResult\\n```\\n\\nReturn types: `str` (auto-converted to user message), `list[Message | str]`, or `PromptResult`. Prompts can be `def` or `async def`. `async def` required when body makes I/O calls (e.g., `operations.view_item()` calls GitHub API).\\n\\n**FastMCP ctx.elicit() API** \u2014 SOURCE: `.claude/worktrees/fastmcp/docs/v2/servers/elicitation.mdx` (accessed 2026-03-06)\\n\\n```python\\nresult = await ctx.elicit(message: str, response_type=<type>)\\n```\\n\\nSupported `response_type` values:\\n- `str`, `int`, `bool` \u2014 scalar primitives\\n- `[\\\"opt1\\\", \\\"opt2\\\"]` \u2014 constrained enum single-select\\n- `[[\\\"opt1\\\", \\\"opt2\\\"]]` \u2014 multi-select\\n- `None` \u2014 confirmation only (no data returned)\\n- `dataclass`, `TypedDict`, `BaseModel` \u2014 structured multi-field; fields must be shallow primitives\\n- `Literal[\\\"a\\\", \\\"b\\\"]` \u2014 enum via Literal type\\n\\n`ElicitationResult` fields: `result.action` (`\\\"accept\\\"` | `\\\"decline\\\"` | `\\\"cancel\\\"`), `result.data` (populated when accept, else None).\\n\\nFor `backlog_add_guided`, structured elicitation dataclass:\\n```python\\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\\nfrom typing import Literal\\n\\n@dataclass\\nclass AddItemRequest:\\n    title: str\\n    priority: Literal[\\\"P0\\\", \\\"P1\\\", \\\"P2\\\", \\\"Ideas\\\"]\\n    description: str\\n    type_: Literal[\\\"Feature\\\", \\\"Bug\\\", \\\"Refactor\\\", \\\"Docs\\\", \\\"Chore\\\"] = \\\"Feature\\\"\\n```\\n\\nClient capability constraint: `ctx.elicit()` raises `NotSupportedError` if the client does not implement an elicitation handler. All guided tools must catch this and return a clear fallback message directing the user to use the non-guided equivalent tool.\\n\\n### Scope\\n\\n**Current state**: server.py contains 10 synchronous `def` tools \u2014 no prompts, no `ctx` usage, no `async def`. The 6 backlog_core files are: `server.py`, `operations.py`, `github.py`, `models.py`, `parsing.py`, `__init__.py`.\\n\\n**New additions to server.py** \u2014 9 new functions total:\\n\\n5 MCP prompts (via `@mcp.prompt` decorator):\\n- `backlog_guide()` \u2014 no parameters, returns full tool reference as messages\\n- `create_item_guided()` \u2014 no parameters, returns intake instructions as messages\\n- `groom_item(selector: str)` \u2014 calls `operations.view_item()` server-side, returns item state + grooming workflow as messages (must be `async def`)\\n- `work_item(selector: str)` \u2014 returns item state + planning instructions as messages (must be `async def`)\\n- `close_or_resolve(selector: str)` \u2014 returns item state + close vs. resolve decision guide as messages (must be `async def`)\\n\\n4 guided tools (via `@mcp.tool`, must be `async def` with `ctx: Context`):\\n- `backlog_add_guided` \u2014 multi-field elicitation then delegates to `operations.add_item()`\\n- `backlog_close_confirmed` \u2014 `response_type=None` elicitation for confirmation before `operations.close_item()`\\n- `backlog_resolve_confirmed` \u2014 structured elicitation for summary before `operations.resolve_item()`\\n- `backlog_setup_github` \u2014 runs label taxonomy and milestone initialization (logic currently in /work-backlog-item setup-github mode)\\n\\n### Skills\\n\\nSkills affected \u2014 logic partially moves to MCP server; skills become thinner wrappers or remain for Claude Code-specific orchestration:\\n\\n- `.claude/skills/backlog/SKILL.md` \u2014 Primary Interface section moves to `backlog_guide()` prompt\\n- `.claude/skills/create-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 interactive intake moves to `backlog_add_guided` + `create_item_guided` prompt; `--auto` mode (codebase research) and `quick` mode stay skill-only\\n- `.claude/skills/groom-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 item state + instructions move to `groom_item` prompt; agent delegation, validity check, PR/commit search stay skill-only\\n- `.claude/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 setup-github and close/resolve confirmation move to `backlog_setup_github`, `backlog_close_confirmed`, `backlog_resolve_confirmed`, `work_item` prompt; SAM planning flow and `--auto` mode stay skill-only\\n\\n## Files\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-05-24T03:29:34.982637Z</sub>\\n\\nPrimary implementation:\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` \u2014 add 5 `@mcp.prompt` functions and 4 async `@mcp.tool` functions with `ctx: Context`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 read-only dependency; `view_item()`, `add_item()`, `close_item()`, `resolve_item()` called by new guided tools\\n\\nSkills to thin after implementation:\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/SKILL.md`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/create-backlog-item/SKILL.md`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/groom-backlog-item/SKILL.md`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md`\\n\\nFastMCP reference (read-only, for implementer):\\n- FastMCP 3.3.x prompts API \u2014 see `/fastmcp-creator:fastmcp-creator` for current reference\\n\\nNote: The `.claude/worktrees/fastmcp/` worktree paths referenced in the original grooming no longer exist. Use the `fastmcp-creator` skill for current FastMCP documentation.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Research\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-05-24T03:45:52.513391Z</sub>\\n\\n<details><summary>struck: 2026-05-24T04:29:54.612630Z \u2014 Replacing March 2026 stale research with three-angle independent research synthesized 2026-05-24</summary>\\n\\n### FastMCP Prompts API (v3.x)\\n\\n**Decorator Syntax**: Use `@mcp.prompt` without parentheses (v3 standard). SOURCE: [gofastmcp/servers/prompts.md](https://gofastmcp.com/servers/prompts.md) (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n**Return Types** (all supported):\\n- `str` \u2014 automatically converted to a single user Message\\n- `list[Message | str]` \u2014 sequences of messages representing a conversation; strings auto-converted to user Messages\\n- `PromptResult` \u2014 full control over messages, description metadata, and custom meta dict\\n\\nExample with PromptResult:\\n```python\\n@mcp.prompt\\ndef code_review(code: str) -> PromptResult:\\n    return PromptResult(\\n        messages=[\\n            Message(f\\\"Please review this code:\\\\n\\\\n```\\\\n{code}\\\\n```\\\"),\\n            Message(\\\"I'll analyze this code for issues.\\\", role=\\\"assistant\\\"),\\n        ],\\n        description=\\\"Code review prompt\\\",\\n        meta={\\\"review_type\\\": \\\"security\\\", \\\"priority\\\": \\\"high\\\"}\\n    )\\n```\\nSOURCE: [gofastmcp/servers/prompts.md](https://gofastmcp.com/servers/prompts.md) (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n**Parameters**: Function parameters define the inputs needed to generate the prompt. MCP clients call prompts with string arguments. Parameter descriptions are extracted from docstrings in Google, NumPy, or Sphinx style and populate the MCP protocol. SOURCE: [gofastmcp/servers/prompts.md](https://gofastmcp.com/servers/prompts.md) (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n### FastMCP Elicitation API (v3.x)\\n\\n**Signature**:\\n```python\\nresult = await ctx.elicit(\\n    \\\"Prompt text to user\\\",\\n    response_type=str,  # or int, bool, list[T], Pydantic model, Literal\\n    response_title=\\\"Optional UI label\\\",  # v3.2.0+\\n    response_description=\\\"Optional UI hint\\\"  # v3.2.0+\\n)\\n```\\nSOURCE: [gofastmcp/servers/elicitation.md](https://gofastmcp.com/servers/elicitation.md) (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n**Supported `response_type` values**:\\n- Scalar types: `str`, `int`, `bool`, `float`\\n- List types: `list[str]`, `list[int]`, or `list[[\\\"option1\\\", \\\"option2\\\", ...]]` for enumerated choices\\n- Pydantic models and dataclasses for structured input\\n- `Literal` types for enum-like constraints\\n- `datetime` (implementation support implied)\\n\\n**ElicitationResult structure**:\\n```python\\nresult.action  # \\\"accept\\\" | \\\"decline\\\" | \\\"cancel\\\"\\nresult.data    # The typed value provided by user (only when action == \\\"accept\\\")\\n```\\nExample:\\n```python\\n@mcp.tool\\nasync def confirm_action(ctx: Context) -> str:\\n    result = await ctx.elicit(\\n        \\\"Confirm this action?\\\",\\n        response_type=bool,\\n        response_title=\\\"Confirmation\\\"\\n    )\\n    if result.action == \\\"accept\\\":\\n        return \\\"Confirmed\\\" if result.data else \\\"Declined\\\"\\n    elif result.action == \\\"decline\\\":\\n        return \\\"User declined\\\"\\n    else:  # cancel\\n        return \\\"Operation cancelled\\\"\\n```\\nSOURCE: [gofastmcp/servers/elicitation.md](https://gofastmcp.com/servers/elicitation.md) (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n**Error Handling**: Elicitation requires the client to implement an elicitation handler. If a client does not support elicitation, `ctx.elicit()` raises an error indicating that elicitation is not supported. No documented `NotSupportedError` exception class name \u2014 error type depends on client implementation. SOURCE: [gofastmcp/servers/elicitation.md](https://gofastmcp.com/servers/elicitation.md) (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n### Context Size and Performance Impact\\n\\n**Server Registration Cost**: Negligible. Prompt and tool registration via decorators is O(1) per function. No measurable impact on server startup time. SOURCE: [gofastmcp/servers/prompts.md](https://gofastmcp.com/servers/prompts.md) (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n**Message List Impact**:\\n- `tools/list` response: Each tool adds 200\u2013500 bytes to JSON payload; 4 new tools \u2248 1\u20132 KB\\n- `prompts/list` response: Each prompt (name + description + argument metadata) \u2248 150\u2013300 bytes; 5 prompts \u2248 1\u20131.5 KB\\n- **Total metadata overhead**: ~2.5\u20133.5 KB to discovery responses\\n\\n**Context Window Consumption**:\\n- Prompts are NOT automatically injected into LLM context. They are only sent when the client requests them.\\n- When a prompt returning a large message list is used, the full message list is injected once at request time.\\n- **Risk**: A prompt returning 100+ messages or messages exceeding 10KB total could significantly bloat context. Design prompts for conciseness.\\n\\n**No documented baseline measurements** for prompt payload size limits or context-at-scale guidance in gofastmcp docs as of 2026-05-24.\\n\\n### MCP Client Compatibility\\n\\n**Prompts Support**: Standard MCP protocol feature. All clients supporting MCP discovery (`prompts/list`) can list prompts. Not all clients expose prompts in UI.\\n\\n**Elicitation Support**: Requires explicit client implementation. Not all MCP clients support elicitation.\\n- **Claude Code**: Supports elicitation (verified in plugin-creator:claude-code-mcp-integration reference)\\n- **Other clients**: Compatibility status not documented in gofastmcp as of 2026-05-24\\n\\n**Fallback Pattern**: Elicitation-dependent tools should document that they require a client with elicitation support. If client lacks support, the tool will raise an error. No graceful degradation mechanism documented. SOURCE: [gofastmcp/servers/elicitation.md](https://gofastmcp.com/servers/elicitation.md) (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n### Community Best Practices\\n\\n**Prompt Design**:\\n1. Keep prompt descriptions short (< 100 chars) to minimize metadata\\n2. For multi-turn flows, use `list[Message]` or `PromptResult` to structure conversation context\\n3. Avoid embedding large datasets in prompt message lists \u2014 use resource/tool parameters instead\\n\\n**Elicitation UX**:\\n1. Use elicitation for confirmations, selections, and structured input (e.g., selecting from a known set of options)\\n2. Do NOT use elicitation for open-ended queries that may not be supported by all clients\\n3. Always handle all three action states: `accept`, `decline`, `cancel`\\n4. Provide `response_title` and `response_description` for UI clarity (v3.2.0+)\\n\\n**Context Management**:\\n1. Prompts should be reusable templates, not full context bundles\\n2. If a prompt needs significant context (e.g., codebase state, large history), consider passing that via tool parameters or resources instead\\n3. Measure actual context injection by enabling MCP logging and observing prompt payload sizes in practice\\n\\nSOURCE: Inferred from [gofastmcp/servers/prompts.md](https://gofastmcp.com/servers/prompts.md), [gofastmcp/servers/elicitation.md](https://gofastmcp.com/servers/elicitation.md), and FastMCP changelog (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n</details>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-05-24T04:29:54.612636Z</sub>\\n\\nResearch conducted 2026-05-24 using three independent angles. Findings that appear across multiple angles are marked **[CROSS-ANGLE]** \u2014 these carry higher confidence.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### FastMCP Prompts API (v3.x current)\\n\\n**Decorator syntax** \u2014 `@mcp.prompt` without parentheses (bare decorator). With overrides: `@mcp.prompt(name=..., description=..., tags=..., enabled=True)`.\\nSOURCE: https://gofastmcp.com/servers/prompts.md (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n**Return types** \u2014 All supported:\\n- `str` \u2014 auto-converted to a single user `Message`\\n- `list[Message | str]` \u2014 sequence of messages; raw strings auto-convert to user `Message`\\n- `PromptResult` \u2014 full control: `messages`, `description` override, custom `meta` dict\\n\\n**Async** \u2014 Both `def` and `async def` supported. Synchronous functions run in a threadpool automatically. `async def` required when the prompt body makes I/O calls (e.g., `operations.view_item()` hits GitHub API).\\n\\n**Context injection** \u2014 `ctx: Context` is injectable as a parameter via FastMCP's dependency injection system. Not passed by the MCP client \u2014 FastMCP resolves it.\\n\\n**[CROSS-ANGLE] All MCP prompt arguments are strings at the protocol layer** \u2014 The MCP spec requires all arguments passed in `prompts/get` to be strings. No integers, no arrays, no JSON types natively. FastMCP works around this by serializing complex types (e.g., `list[int]`) as JSON strings and embedding a schema hint in the `description` field. Clients passing an integer `42` instead of the string `\\\"42\\\"` will produce undefined behaviour.\\nSOURCE: https://gofastmcp.com/servers/prompts.md + https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/concepts/prompts.md (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n**Enabled/disabled** \u2014 Prompts can be enabled/disabled at runtime. Disabled prompts do not appear in `prompts/list`. FastMCP sends `notifications/prompts/list_changed` automatically when prompts change.\\n\\n**`listChanged` capability gate** \u2014 The server must declare `\\\"prompts\\\": {\\\"listChanged\\\": true}` during initialization for clients to act on list-change notifications. Clients that don't declare this capability will not re-fetch `prompts/list` dynamically.\\nSOURCE: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/concepts/prompts.md (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n**v2 \u2192 v3 breaking changes relevant to this item**:\\n- `mcp.types.PromptMessage` replaced by `fastmcp.prompts.Message` \u2014 old import raises `ImportError`\\n- Returning `{\\\"role\\\": \\\"user\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"...\\\"}` dicts from a prompt no longer silently coerced \u2014 raises error; must use `Message` objects\\n- `on_duplicate_prompts` constructor kwarg removed; replaced by unified `on_duplicate=`\\nSOURCE: https://gofastmcp.com/getting-started/upgrading/from-fastmcp-2.md (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### FastMCP Elicitation API (v3.x current \u2014 installed 3.2.4)\\n\\n**Full signature** (from `fastmcp/server/context.py` lines 1102\u20131121, installed source):\\n\\n```python\\nasync def elicit(\\n    self,\\n    message: str,\\n    response_type: type[T]\\n        | list[str]\\n        | dict[str, dict[str, str]]\\n        | list[list[str]]\\n        | list[dict[str, dict[str, str]]]\\n        | None = None,\\n    *,\\n    response_title: str | None = None,      # v3.3.0+\\n    response_description: str | None = None, # v3.3.0+\\n) -> AcceptedElicitation[T] | DeclinedElicitation | CancelledElicitation\\n```\\n\\n`response_type=None` is **deprecated** as of 3.2.4 \u2014 causes VS Code to render an empty, non-functional form.\\n\\n**Supported `response_type` values**:\\n- Scalar: `str`, `int`, `float`, `bool`\\n- `Literal[...]`, `Enum` subclass\\n- Single-select list: `[\\\"a\\\", \\\"b\\\", \\\"c\\\"]` (strings) or `{\\\"key\\\": {\\\"title\\\": \\\"Label\\\"}}` (titled)\\n- Multi-select: `[[\\\"a\\\", \\\"b\\\"]]` or `[{\\\"key\\\": {\\\"title\\\": \\\"Label\\\"}}]`\\n- `list[X]` type annotation\\n- `BaseModel`, `dataclass`, `TypedDict` \u2014 structured multi-field (must be flat primitives per MCP spec)\\nSOURCE: `fastmcp/server/elicitation.py` installed 3.2.4 (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n**ElicitationResult** \u2014 three distinct types:\\n- `AcceptedElicitation[T]` \u2014 `action=\\\"accept\\\"`, `data` = validated response value\\n- `DeclinedElicitation` \u2014 `action=\\\"decline\\\"`, no data\\n- `CancelledElicitation` \u2014 `action=\\\"cancel\\\"`, no data\\nSOURCE: `mcp/server/elicitation.py` installed alongside fastmcp 3.2.4 (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n**[CROSS-ANGLE] Exception class when client does not support elicitation \u2014 `McpError`, NOT `NotSupportedError`**:\\n\\nThe March 2026 grooming named `NotSupportedError`. This is incorrect. Source inspection of the installed mcp SDK confirms:\\n\\n- The MCP SDK default elicitation callback returns `ErrorData(code=INVALID_REQUEST, message=\\\"Elicitation not supported\\\")`\\n- The SDK's `send_request` translates `ErrorData` responses into `McpError`\\n- `NotSupportedError` does not exist in fastmcp 3.2.4 or the mcp SDK \u2014 confirmed by searching all exception definitions in both packages\\n\\nCorrect catch pattern:\\n```python\\nfrom mcp import McpError\\n\\ntry:\\n    result = await ctx.elicit(\\\"Confirm?\\\", response_type=bool)\\nexcept McpError as e:\\n    # e.error.code == -32600 (INVALID_REQUEST)\\n    # str(e) == \\\"Elicitation not supported\\\"\\n    return \\\"Client does not support elicitation \u2014 use non-guided equivalent\\\"\\n```\\nSOURCE: `mcp/client/session.py` lines 74\u201383, `mcp/shared/exceptions.py`, `fastmcp/exceptions.py` installed 3.2.4 (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n**MCP schema constraint** \u2014 Elicitation schemas must be flat objects with only primitive scalar properties. FastMCP enforces this via `validate_elicitation_json_schema()` which raises `ValueError` at request time if violated. Nested objects, arrays of non-primitives, and complex types are rejected.\\n\\n**Known gotcha \u2014 VS Code empty form hang**: `response_type=None` causes VS Code to hang. Noted directly in fastmcp 3.2.4 source deprecation warning.\\nSOURCE: `fastmcp/server/context.py` lines 1163\u20131167 installed 3.2.4 (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n**Changelog highlights**:\\n- v2.10.0 \u2014 first introduction of elicitation support\\n- v2.12.0 / v2.14.2 / v3.0.0b1 \u2014 enum elicitation schema compliance fixes (multiple rounds)\\n- v3.0.0b2 / rc1 \u2014 background task elicitation via Redis coordination\\n- v3.3.0 \u2014 `response_title` and `response_description` parameters added\\nSOURCE: https://gofastmcp.com/changelog.md (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Context Size and Performance Impact (measured)\\n\\n**Current server.py baseline**:\\n- 40 tools registered, 185,564 bytes\\n- Estimated `tools/list` payload: ~34,360 bytes (~33.5 KB)\\n- After #473 (+4 guided tools): ~37,796 bytes \u2014 delta ~3.4 KB. Negligible.\\n\\n**`backlog_guide()` injection cost** (SKILL.md measured):\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/SKILL.md`: **14,976 chars, 277 lines**\\n- Estimated tokens: ~3,744\u20134,279 per call\\n- At 32k context: ~12% of total context per call \u2014 borderline for small context windows\\n- At 128k+ context: ~3% \u2014 acceptable\\n- **[CROSS-ANGLE] Prompts are NOT auto-injected** \u2014 only when client explicitly calls `backlog_guide()`. This is a caller-controlled cost, not a passive tax per session.\\n\\n**`groom_item()` / `work_item()` injection cost** (representative items measured across 543 active items):\\n- Median item: ~4,369 bytes / ~1,090 tokens\\n- P90 item: ~27,991 bytes / ~7,000 tokens\\n- Item #473 (heavily worked): ~46,424 bytes / ~11,600 tokens\\n- **Risk**: Large items in the tail will inject 7k\u201312k tokens. The existing server `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET` gate in `backlog_view` limits view responses above ~16k chars \u2014 the prompt implementation MUST apply the same budget gate before injecting into the message, or `groom_item()` on a large item will exceed client context budgets.\\n\\n**`prompts/list` growth**:\\n- 5 new prompts \u00d7 ~250 bytes each = ~1.1\u20131.7 KB delta. Trivial.\\n\\n**[CROSS-ANGLE] No published performance benchmarks** \u2014 Neither fastmcp GitHub nor the MCP spec document startup time or `tools/list` response latency at scale. No measurements available for the 40\u219244 tool delta beyond estimation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### MCP Client Compatibility\\n\\n**Prompts**: Any client supporting `prompts/list` can list prompts. Clients must explicitly declare the `prompts` capability. Injection behaviour after `prompts/get` is **client-defined** \u2014 the MCP spec does not specify how clients use the returned messages. Different clients (Claude Desktop, VS Code, Cursor, Zed) handle prompt messages differently. Verified for Claude Code; unverified for others.\\nSOURCE: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/concepts/prompts.md (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n**Elicitation**: Requires explicit client-side `elicitation_handler` registration. Client must declare `elicitation` capability during initialization. Without the handler, the SDK returns `ErrorData(INVALID_REQUEST)` which surfaces as `McpError` in tool code.\\n- **VS Code**: Partial \u2014 `response_type=None` causes hang (confirmed from fastmcp source)\\n- **Claude Code**: Confirmed via `plugin-creator:claude-code-mcp-integration` reference\\n- **Claude Desktop, Cursor, Zed**: No compatibility statement found in gofastmcp.com docs as of 2026-05-24\\nSOURCE: `mcp/client/session.py` installed 3.2.4; https://gofastmcp.com/servers/elicitation.md (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Security Note\\n\\nMCP spec flags: \\\"Implementations MUST carefully validate all prompt inputs and outputs to prevent injection attacks.\\\" `backlog_guide()` injects the full SKILL.md \u2014 which contains agent instructions \u2014 directly into client context as a message. If the SKILL.md content can be modified by an attacker, prompt injection into the LLM context is possible. The implementation should treat the SKILL.md read as a trusted internal resource, not user-supplied content.\\nSOURCE: MCP specification, server/prompts section (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Community Best Practices (sourced from primary docs, changelog patterns, and source code comments)\\n\\n1. Keep prompt descriptions short (< 100 chars) to minimise `prompts/list` metadata\\n2. Use `list[Message]` or `PromptResult` for multi-turn flows\\n3. For large context payloads (like `backlog_guide()`), consider whether the content can be split into a resource instead of a prompt message\\n4. Always handle all three elicitation action states: `accept`, `decline`, `cancel`\\n5. Never use `response_type=None` \u2014 deprecated, breaks VS Code\\n6. Provide `response_title` and `response_description` for UI clarity (v3.3.0+, requires fastmcp>=3.3.0)\\n7. Apply `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET` equivalent before injecting dynamic item content in `groom_item()` / `work_item()`\\nSOURCE: https://gofastmcp.com/servers/prompts.md, https://gofastmcp.com/servers/elicitation.md, fastmcp changelog (accessed 2026-05-24)\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Unresolved / Gaps\\n\\n- No per-client elicitation compatibility matrix from the FastMCP docs \u2014 only VS Code and FastMCP Python Client are documented\\n- No protocol-level ceiling on prompt payload size \u2014 practical limit is the consuming LLM's context window\\n- Whether `backlog_guide()` SKILL.md will be cached at server startup or re-read per call is an implementation decision not yet specified \u2014 affects memory footprint vs. I/O cost\\n- GitHub issue search was rate-limited \u2014 real-world failure reports beyond what appears in the changelog were not retrievable\\n</div>\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-05-24T03:29:09.482243Z</sub>\\n\\n**Blocked by:**\\n\\n_No longer blocked._ Both prerequisites are confirmed implemented:\\n\\n- **#472** \u2014 CLOSED. Commits: `a89eb9d9` (convert backlog_core tools to async def with asyncio.to_thread()) and `74a913ed` (mark #472 async conversion tasks complete and resolve). Async tool patterns and asyncio.to_thread() wrapping are now available in server.py.\\n- **#465** \u2014 CLOSED. Commits: `895d61e3` (post-implementation annotations and doc updates for context-logging-progress) and `a89eb9d9`. Context logging and progress reporting are implemented.\\n\\n**Sequencing concern \u2014 #2292:**\\n\\n`#2292` (\\\"refactor: Backlog MCP server exposes 42 flat tools instead of consolidated action-based pattern\\\", open, in-progress) restructures `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` from 40+ flat tools to 4 consolidated action-discriminated tools. The new prompts and guided tools in #473 add to server.py.\\n\\nIf #2292 lands first: #473's new prompts/guided tools attach to the consolidated server structure. The guided tools (backlog_add_guided, etc.) become elicitation variants of the consolidated tool actions, not standalone flat tools.\\nIf #473 lands before #2292: the #2292 consolidation must preserve the new prompt registrations and async guided tools during the restructure.\\n\\nDecision on sequencing belongs in #2292's planning. Recorded here as a coordination note.\\n\\n**Blocks:** Nothing currently in backlog.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Artifact Manifest\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n<!-- artifact-manifest:begin -->\\n| Type | Path | Status | Agent | Created |\\n|------|------|--------|-------|---------|\\n| task-plan | plan/P001-mcp-prompts-elicitation.yaml | current | dh_migrate | 2026-03-24T16:33:15Z |\\n| architect | plan/architect-mcp-prompts-elicitation.md | current | dh_migrate | 2026-03-24T16:39:46Z |\\n| feature-context | plan/feature-context-mcp-prompts-elicitation.md | current | dh_migrate | 2026-03-24T16:43:40Z |\\n<!-- artifact-manifest:end -->\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: GitHub Issue #473\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-03-06\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-03-06T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer**, I want **The workflow logic currently scattered across /backlog, /create-backlog-item,...** so that **backlog items are tracked in GitHub**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_guide` \u2014 new `@mcp.prompt` providing guided elicitation for general backlog workflows; no existing code changed, purely additive\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::create_item_guided` \u2014 new `@mcp.prompt` for step-by-step item creation; no existing code changed\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::groom_item` \u2014 new `@mcp.prompt` for grooming guidance; no existing code changed\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::work_item` \u2014 new `@mcp.prompt` for work-item guidance; no existing code changed\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::close_or_resolve` \u2014 new `@mcp.prompt` for close/re